# Keeps the build context (and therefore the final image) limited to what
# the app actually needs to run. Two concrete problems this closes:
#
# 1. Secrets: with no .dockerignore, `COPY . .` in the Dockerfile bakes
#    whatever's on disk straight into an image layer — including a real
#    backend/.env (YOUTUBE_API_KEY, APIFY_API_TOKEN, DATABASE_URL) if one
#    exists locally when `docker build` is run, exactly as
#    PRODUCTION_ROADMAP.md's self-hosted "Path B" quickstart has someone do
#    from this directory. An image layer with a secret in it doesn't stop
#    being a leak just because the final CMD never reads that file again —
#    anyone who can pull or inspect the image can still extract it.
# 2. Bloat/noise: without this, the build context also swept up the local
#    .venv/, test suite, pytest cache, and docs — none of which the running
#    container needs, and all of which just make the image bigger and the
#    build slower.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.git/
.gitignore
tests/
docs/
README.md
*.md
